1) What is Zone Redunse scale set how it is works 

Zone-Redundant VM Scale Set (Short Note)

A Zone-Redundant Virtual Machine Scale Set (VMSS) distributes virtual machine instances across multiple Availability Zones within the same Azure region to provide high availability and fault tolerance.

For example, if the minimum instance count is 3 and three availability zones are selected, Azure places one VM in each zone. When traffic increases, VMSS automatically scales out by creating additional VMs and distributes them evenly across the zones. If the scale set reaches 9 instances, Azure typically places 3 VMs in each zone.

This design ensures that if one availability zone fails, the VMs in the remaining zones continue serving traffic, minimizing downtime and improving application reliability.


2)How Azure is going to calculate the cost if u are trying to implement the vm scale set 
How Azure Calculates the Cost for a VM Scale Set (VMSS)

Azure does not charge separately for VMSS itself. You pay for the resources used by the instances inside the scale set.

The cost is calculated based on:

Number of VM Instances
More VMs = Higher cost
Example:
3 VMs → Pay for 3 VMs
9 VMs → Pay for 9 VMs
Example

Suppose:

VM Size = Standard_B2s
Min Instances = 3
Max Instances = 9

Normal Traffic:

3 VMs Running
Cost = Cost of 3 VMs + Disks + Networking

Peak Traffic:

9 VMs Running
Cost = Cost of 9 VMs + Disks + Networking

If autoscaling keeps 9 VMs running for only 2 hours, you pay for those additional 6 VMs only during those 2 hours.

